# ---- build outputs -------------------------------------------------------
# Artifact names carry $(VERSION_TAG), so match the stem rather than a version.
gen/
PS5-Nexus-*.elf
PS5-Nexus-linux-*
PS5-Nexus-asan
linux-obj/
ps5-obj/
*.o
*.d

# ---- local scratch -------------------------------------------------------
# Host-test sandboxes, fixtures, backups and screenshots. Never publish: the
# backups alone are tens of MB and they contain point-in-time copies of src/.
# NOTE: ignore the CONTENTS (.build/*), not the directory itself — git never
# descends into an excluded directory, so "!.build/x" on top of ".build/"
# can never re-include anything.
.build/*
# Whitelist: the front-end acceptance harness (vm-level checks pinned to
# assets/index.html) is a real deliverable, not scratch.
!.build/dock_pkg_checks.mjs
# Upload same-name conflict acceptance (routes /api/upload-file, 11 assertions)
# + generic inline-script syntax checker used by it.
!.build/chk_upload.mjs
!.build/chk_inline.mjs
# Deep-tree save backup crash repro (SIGSEGV at copy_tree_r depth 13, the
# 68KB-frame LTO-inlining regression). One-liner triage for future stack
# regressions: wsl.exe -d Ubuntu-22.04 bash .build/repro-deep-backup.sh
!.build/repro-deep-backup.sh
# Static-guard driver: the ONE list of chk_*.py guards + their counter-proofs,
# plus two self-checks (registry parity against build-verify-wsl.sh, and
# "the whole suite must leave assets/ + src/ byte-identical"). Guards used to be
# registered separately in each build script; when one script superseded another
# it copied the list and dropped chk_signal_disposition.py, which then stopped
# running and nobody noticed. One list + a parity check is the fix.
!.build/run-guards.sh
# Front-end gate stamp helper. `--write` records that the Chromium suite and the
# save-page counter-proofs passed for a specific set of front-end bytes;
# `--check` lets the build ask "was the gate run for THIS tree?" instead of
# echoing a line nobody updates (that line still said "196 assertions" at 214).
!.build/frontend_stamp.py
# ⚠️ Deliberately NOT whitelisted: .build/.frontend-gate-ok is the per-run
# record those two produce -- it is state, not source.

# ---- guard suite: deliverable, not scratch -------------------------------
# 2026-10-16: of the 127 scripts under .build/, exactly TWO were tracked --
# every guard, every counter-proof, every verification entry point lived only in
# the working copy. That is the deeper half of the "guards rot silently" problem:
# a guard that is not in the repo cannot be run on a fresh clone at all, and
# `make test` would fail with "file not found" instead of telling you anything.
# Whitelisted by family (screenshots/backups/probes stay ignored):
!.build/chk_*.py
!.build/chk-*-counterproof.py
!.build/savemgr-game-counterproof.py
!.build/saves-ui-counterproof.py
# ⚠️ By family, not by name. The first pass listed .sh drivers one by one and
#    immediately missed two new ones (*-counterproof-wsl.sh) plus every *_test.c
#    -- including sfo_unit_test.c, which had never been tracked. Every .sh and
#    .c under .build/ is a test driver or a unit-test TU; there is no scratch
#    of those types here (scratch is preview/, shots/, logs/ -- still ignored).
!.build/*.sh
!.build/*.c
# The offline preview harness the front-end gate drives (preview_build.py is
# also what keeps a re-run from silently wiping the injected fetch stub).
!.build/preview_build.py
!.build/preview_check.mjs
!.build/preview_stub.js
# hosttest.py points WFM_SAVE_LOG at the repo root and re-creates it on every
# run; it is a per-run forensics dump, never a deliverable.
save-delete.log

# ---- pre-rename artifacts -----------------------------------------------
# Kept listed so an old binary lying around cannot be mistaken for a fresh
# build (a stale NEXUS.elf is exactly how "the update did nothing" happens).
NEXUS.elf
NEXUS-linux
NEXUS-asan
web-file-mgr.elf
web-file-mgr-linux

# ---- embedded PC helper -------------------------------------------------
# serve-pkg.py IS embedded in the ELF (via .incbin); serve-pkg.exe is NOT —
# it is ~9.2 MB of bundled CPython and ships via the GitHub Release instead.
# Never let either leak back into the repo.
assets/serve-pkg.exe
*.exe

